Subject: [PATCH 4/4] R80b, part 6, patch 4 (cld-advisor, at main's word in
 M18; the third gap of the cold read of part 1): A TEXT CUT FOR A CELL KEEPS A
 WORD OF IT

The plan's builder cuts a long text for a table cell at a word's end and marks it as cut. Part 1 of this
series mended a text that opens with punctuation and came back as the mark alone. The cold read of that part
found three texts that still come back with nothing of the sentence in them. Main gives them in M18 and has
not checked them. Made again here, with the function as it stood kept in the test:

  - a text that opens with a bracket or a dash and then holds only spaces, commas, semicolons or colons up
    to the cut: the mark alone;
  - a text that opens with a comma and spaces: the comma and the spaces;
  - a short label in brackets before a long word: the label alone.

The mend: where the cut at a word's end keeps less than half of what a cell may hold, or no letter and no
digit, the text is cut at the cell's length, inside the long word; and where the first characters up to
that length hold no letter and no digit at all, the text is kept to the end of its first word, to three
times the cell's length at most. The lines that stood are kept in the builder under a dated note.

Twins: an ordinary sentence, a text that fits, one long word, a run of short words and a text that opens
with a space each come out as they did. A text with no word in it at all is cut at the cell's length.

No cell of the plan's page changes: the builder's output is the committed page. The lock passes: 22 tests.
Not checked by main. Tested against planted texts by the session that wrote the mend: four, with five twins.
